Article 12 An appraisal professional shall enjoy the following rights:
(1) To require the client to provide relevant ownership certificates, financial accounting information and other materials, as well as the necessary assistance required for performing fair appraisal procedures;
(2) To consult, according to law, documents, certificates and materials needed for conducting business from relevant State organs or other organizations;
(3) To refuse illegal interference by the client or other organizations or individuals in the appraisal activities and appraisal results;
(4) To sign appraisal reports according to law;
(5) Other rights provided by laws and administrative regulations.

Article 13 An appraisal professional shall perform the following duties:
(1) To act in good faith and conduct business independently, objectively and impartially according to law;
(2) To comply with appraisal standards, perform investigation duties, independently analyze and estimate, and conduct business diligently and carefully;
(3) To complete required continuing education, and maintain and enhance professional competence;
(4) To verify and validate the authenticity, accuracy and completeness of relevant documents, certificates and materials used in appraisal activities;
(5) To keep confidential any State secrets, trade secrets and personal privacy learned during appraisal activities;
(6) To withdraw if there is a conflict of interest with the client or other relevant parties or the appraisal subject;
(7) To accept the self-discipline management of the trade association and perform the duties stipulated in the articles of association of the trade association;
(8) Other duties provided by laws and administrative regulations.

Article 14 An appraisal professional shall not engage in any of the following acts:
(1) Accepting an engagement to conduct business and collect fees without authorization;
(2) Conducting business in two or more appraisal institutions simultaneously;
(3) Soliciting business by improper means such as deception, inducement, coercion, or disparaging or defaming other appraisal professionals;
(4) Allowing another person to conduct business in his or her name, or impersonating another person to conduct business;
(5) Signing an appraisal report for a business that he or she did not undertake;
(6) Demanding, accepting, or demanding or accepting in disguised form, any remuneration or property other than as agreed in the contract, or seeking other improper benefits;
(7) Signing a false appraisal report or an appraisal report with material omissions;
(8) Other acts violating laws or administrative regulations.

Article 20 An appraisal institution shall not engage in any of the following acts:
(1) Taking advantage of its business to seek improper benefits;
(2) Allowing another institution to conduct business in its name, or impersonating another institution to conduct business;
(3) Soliciting business by improper means such as malicious price reduction, paying kickbacks, false publicity, or disparaging or defaming other appraisal institutions;
(4) Accepting business in which it has a conflict of interest;
(5) Engaging in appraisal of the same appraisal subject by separately accepting engagements from parties with conflicting interests;
(6) Issuing a false appraisal report or an appraisal report with material omissions;
(7) Employing or assigning persons who do not meet the requirements of this Law to engage in appraisal business;
(8) Other acts violating laws or administrative regulations.

Article 36 An appraisal trade association shall perform the following duties:
(1) To formulate self-discipline management measures for members and exercise self-discipline management over members;
(2) To formulate professional practice standards and professional ethics standards based on the basic appraisal standards;
(3) To organize and carry out continuing education for members;
(4) To establish credit files for members, record in the credit files the members' compliance with laws, administrative regulations and appraisal standards, and disclose such information to the public;
(5) To inspect the establishment of risk prevention mechanisms by members;
(6) To accept complaints and reports against members, accept petitions from members, and mediate professional disputes among members;
(7) To regulate the professional conduct of members, regularly inspect the appraisal reports issued by members, give rewards or impose penalties on members in accordance with the articles of association, and promptly report the rewards and penalties to the relevant appraisal administrative department;
(8) To ensure that members conduct business according to law and protect the lawful rights and interests of members;
(9) Other duties provided by laws, administrative regulations and the articles of association.

Article 44 Where an appraisal professional, in violation of the provisions of this Law, falls under any of the following circumstances, the relevant appraisal administrative department shall issue a warning and may order a suspension from practice for not less than six months but not more than one year; if there are illegal gains, the illegal gains shall be confiscated; if the circumstances are serious, a suspension from practice for not less than one year but not more than five years shall be ordered; if a crime is constituted, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law:
(1) Accepting an engagement to conduct business and collect fees without authorization;
(2) Conducting business in two or more appraisal institutions simultaneously;
(3) Soliciting business by improper means such as deception, inducement, coercion, or disparaging or defaming other appraisal professionals;
(4) Allowing another person to conduct business in his or her name, or impersonating another person to conduct business;
(5) Signing an appraisal report for a business that he or she did not undertake or an appraisal report with material omissions;
(6) Demanding, accepting, or demanding or accepting in disguised form, any remuneration or property other than as agreed in the contract, or seeking other improper benefits.

Article 47 Where an appraisal institution, in violation of the provisions of this Law, falls under any of the following circumstances, the relevant appraisal administrative department shall issue a warning and may order suspension of business for not less than one month but not more than six months; if there are illegal gains, the illegal gains shall be confiscated, and a fine of not less than one time but not more than five times the illegal gains shall also be imposed; if the circumstances are serious, the administrative department for industry and commerce shall revoke the business license; if a crime is constituted, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law:
(1) Taking advantage of its business to seek improper benefits;
(2) Allowing another institution to conduct business in its name, or impersonating another institution to conduct business;
(3) Soliciting business by improper means such as malicious price reduction, paying kickbacks, false publicity, or disparaging or defaming other appraisal institutions;
(4) Accepting business in which it has a conflict of interest;
(5) Engaging in appraisal of the same appraisal subject by separately accepting engagements from parties with conflicting interests;
(6) Issuing an appraisal report with material omissions;
(7) Failing to retain appraisal archives for the period provided in this Law;
(8) Employing or assigning persons who do not meet the requirements of this Law to engage in appraisal business;
(9) Negligently managing its appraisal professionals, resulting in adverse consequences.
Where an appraisal institution fails to file a record as required by this Law or does not meet the conditions set forth in Article 15 of this Law, the relevant appraisal administrative department shall order it to make corrections; if it refuses to make corrections, it shall be ordered to suspend business, and may also be fined not less than RMB 10,000 but not more than RMB 50,000.

Article 52 Where, in violation of the provisions of this Law, a client falls under any of the following circumstances in a statutory appraisal, the relevant appraisal administrative department, together with the relevant departments, shall order rectification; if the client refuses to rectify, a fine of not less than RMB 100,000 but not more than RMB 500,000 shall be imposed; if there are illegal gains, the illegal gains shall be confiscated; if the circumstances are serious, the directly responsible supervisor and other directly liable persons shall be given sanctions according to law; if losses are caused, compensation liability shall be borne according to law; if a crime is constituted, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law:
(1) Failing to select an appraisal institution according to law;
(2) Demanding, accepting, or demanding or accepting in disguised form, any kickback;
(3) Colluding with or instigating an appraisal institution or appraiser to issue a false appraisal report;
(4) Failing to truthfully provide the appraisal institution with ownership certificates, financial accounting information and other materials;
(5) Failing to use the appraisal report in accordance with the provisions of law and the scope of use specified in the appraisal report.
Where a client other than the one specified in the preceding paragraph violates the provisions of this Law and causes losses to others, it shall bear compensation liability according to law.
